LRC as a cascade of conditional clearances; the cycle-exclusion crux (oracle-S545)

(1) CASCADE  |SAFE| = prod c_i  (conditional clearance of runner i | previous)
   generic n=5: c_i = ['3/5', '5/9', '13/25', '9/13']  -> prod = 3/25 (= |SAFE|)
   AP n=5 (tight): c_i = ['3/5', '2/3', '1/3', '0']  -> prod = 0 (= |SAFE|)
   (a zero factor = the runner whose clearance the previous ones made impossible.)

(2) WHICH ORDER does the cycle-exclusion live at? The cycle-exclusion is a
    TRIPLE (3-node) fact, i.e. a Helly-3 condition: does 'every triple clears'
    force 'all clear'? Compare full collar vs worst k-subset collar.
    system           1/n   full worst pair worst triple worst 4-set
    AP n=5           1/5    1/5       3/10          1/4         1/5
    AP n=6           1/6    1/6       7/24          1/4        7/36
    AP n=7           1/7    1/7        2/7          1/4        4/21
    generic n=6      1/6    1/3       7/24          1/3         1/3
    => worst k-subset collar = 1/(k+1) (the k-runner LRC, achieved by the sub-AP):
       worst TRIPLE collar = 1/4 for AP n=5,6,7; the FULL collar = 1/n. So clearing
       every triple (cycle-exclusion / Helly-3) gives only 1/4, NOT 1/n.

READING: the cascade telescopes |SAFE| = prod of conditional clearances; a tight
system has a ZERO factor (the runner the previous clearances trapped, at the wall).
The user's cycle-exclusion (no 3-cycle) is the TRIPLE / Helly-3 layer -- REAL and
necessary (every triple of runners clears, collar 1/4 > 1/n) but NOT sufficient: the
collar DEGRADES monotonically with subset size (1/(k+1)), reaching 1/n only at the
FULL cascade. So LRC is irreducibly the whole cascade; the cycle-exclusion is its
3-shadow. (The naive 'tournament 3-cycle at t* <=> inside debt' fails: t* is a wall
where the half-turn tournament has ties, so the 3-cycle there is ill-defined.)
